{
  "title": "Snapping Extensor Carpi Ulnaris (ECU)",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/hand/6030/snapping-extensor-carpi-ulnaris-ecu",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Apr 2 2026",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "Summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Snapping ECU is a clinical condition characterized by pain over the ulnar wrist caused by instability and tendonitis of the ECU tendon secondary overuse.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis is made with clinical examination with palpation of the ECU tendon and noting a painful snap while moving the wrist from pronation to supination.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is usually rest and wrist splinting. In refractory cases ECU subsheath reconstruction may be considered.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "4",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Incidence",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "5",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "common with sporting activities",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Risk factors",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "tennis",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "golf",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "rupby",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Pathoanatomy",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "ECU subluxation is secondary to attenuation or **rupture of the ECU subsheath (6th dorsal compartment)**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**attenuation**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/6030/images/attenuate.jpg",
                  "caption": "subsheath attenuation"
                }
              ],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "remains intact but is stripped at ulnar/palmar attachment to produce a false pouch that the ECU tendon can subluxate/dislocate into",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**rupture**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/6030/images/torn.jpg",
                  "caption": "Ulnar sided tears (top) result in dislocation of the tendon in supination, and relocation upon pronation. Radial sided tears (bottom), the tendon ends up on top of the torn subsheath following relocation/pronation."
                }
              ],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "ulnar sided ECU subsheath tears",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "ECU subluxates on supination, and reduces on pronation",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "radial sided ECU subsheath tears",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "ECU subluxates on supination, and lies **on top of the torn subsheath** on pronation",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "subluxation and snapping can lead to ECU tendonitis",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Anatomy",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Extensor tendon compartments",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/6030/images/wrist_compartments1500_moved.jpg",
              "caption": ""
            }
          ],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Compartment 1 (De Quervain's Tenosynovitis )",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "APL",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "EBP",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**Compartment 2 (Intersection syndrome )**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "ECRL",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "ECRB",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "Compartment 3",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "EPL",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "Compartment 4",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "EIP",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "EDC",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**Compartment 5 (Vaughan-Jackson Syndrome )**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "EDM",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**Compartment 6 (Snapping ECU )**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "ECU",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "ECU tendon",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "ECU tendon inserts onto the 5th metacarpal base",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**ECU subsheath** is part of the TFCC that is most critical to ECU stability",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/6030/images/subsheath.jpg",
                  "caption": "subsheath (red)"
                },
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/6030/images/subsheath_axial_cut.jpg",
                  "caption": "subsheath axial cut"
                }
              ],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "ECU lies dorsal during supination and translates ulnar during pronation",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "in pronation, the ECU tendon exits the wrist in a straight direction.",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "in supination, the tendon exits the sixth compartment at an angle of approximately 30°",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "tension on the ECU retinaculum and subsheath is therefore greater during activities involving supination",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/6030/images/0050ce3f-9f72-4d7f-af3b-1e43ee45eef7_ecu_position_with_supination_pronation..jpg",
                      "caption": "ECU motion"
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "ECU subluxates during ulnar deviation, **supination**, wrist flexion",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/6030/images/rotation.jpg",
                  "caption": "ECU during pronation (left, straight coures) and supination (right, ECU forced into forming 30&#176; angle with ulna at level of ECU subsheath)"
                }
              ],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "this position has the greatest angulation of the ECU tendon with respect to the ulna",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Presentation",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Symptoms",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**pain and snapping** over dorsal ulnar wrist",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Physical exam",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**flexion and supination** of the wrist elicit a painful snap",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**ECU tendon reduces with pronation**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Pain at ulnar wrist with ECU synergy test may be indicative of tendonitis",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
